# The vocabularies Nix parses should be types, not bare strings

**DONE.** Closed before these files carried a status word. The `.done`
suffix is the record; this line is that same answer in the second place
the check reads, and no new judgement.

Carl, 2026-08-25: "I'd like to make a stringenum for things that are
stringenums if possible without ruining codegen."

## Where it stands

`Store.add_to_store` takes `method: str` and `hash_algo: str`. Both are
closed sets that libstore parses - `flat`, `nar`, `git`, `text` and
`blake3`, `md5`, `sha1`, `sha256`, `sha512` - and a Python developer
calling this has nothing to tell them so. An invented value fails at
the call, with a good message, which is the wrong end of the loop: an
editor should have offered the four.

## Can it live in a pxd or a pyx?

Carl asked. The answer is yes for a pyx and no for a pxd, and neither
is where it ended up.

A `StrEnum` compiles inside a `.pyx` and works completely - probed,
not assumed: real `EnumType` metaclass, `isinstance(member, str)`,
`str()` gives the value, lookup and rejection both correct. Cython
runs the metaclass and `__prepare__` like any other Python class.

A `.pxd` cannot. A pxd declares C-level things for `cimport`, and a
StrEnum is a Python object with a metaclass - there is nothing to
declare. Cython's `cpdef enum` IS pxd-able and is the wrong type: it
is int-valued, and this vocabulary is strings.

It went in a plain `.py` anyway, beside `errors.py`. Compiling a
vocabulary into a shared object buys nothing - no C++ is involved -
and a `.py` module needs no generated stub for a typechecker to read
it, because `py.typed` says `partial`. The one thing a pyx would add
is deriving the values from libstore at import, and that trades static
member visibility for rename-detection the round-trip test already
gives.

## Why a StrEnum is the right shape

A `StrEnum` member IS a str at runtime. So NOTHING about the transport
changes: the field stays a protobuf `string`, the codec still sends a
string, and no schema or wire question arises. The whole cost is the
codegen recognising the type name.

That is also why it beats `Literal`: the value is still a plain string
wherever one is wanted, but there is a class to attach the vocabulary
to, and a decoded value can come back TYPED rather than as a bare str.

## What it touches

Discovery needs no new declaration, unlike `_errors_module`. The
classes live in the bindings package and are found the way wrapper
classes are - walk `dir(huggorm_bindings)` for `type` objects that
subclass both `str` and `Enum`. So adding a vocabulary means adding it
to the bindings and nothing else.

From there it is one table in the manifest and four readers:

- `grpc_schema._msg_arg_type` and `wire_blocker`: an enum name is the
  `string` scalar.
- `wire.py`'s `kind()`: scalar. Its converter should be the enum CLASS
  rather than `str`, so a value decoded from the wire arrives typed.
  `str(member)` is the value on the way out, so encoding needs nothing.
- the emitter: already works. `_huggorm_bindings_import` emits
  `from huggorm_bindings import X` for any annotated name that is not
  a builtin, so exporting the enum from `__init__` is enough.
- `stub_module`: I called this the obstacle and overstated it. `home`
  is a plain dict mapping type name to module, so adding the enums to
  it is two lines and `store.pyi` gets `from .content_address import
  ContentAddressMethod` for free. `__init__.pyi` needed one more,
  because it must re-export exactly what the package exports - and a
  gate already held that, which is how it was found.

## Checking the values are Nix's

A TEST, not a build-time shim. Parametrize over each member and push it
through `add_to_store` against the chroot store: a real round trip
through libstore, hermetic, and it needs no new C++ and no new
declaration.

It cannot catch a member Nix ADDS. C++ has no reflection, so any list
of enum members here is hand-written whether it lives in Python or in a
shim - the check catches a typo, a rename and a removal, and that is
what it should claim.

## Naming

`ContentAddressMethod` and `HashAlgorithm`, matching the C++ types
`addToStoreFromDump` takes. No `Nix` prefix.

## What it cost, in the end

Four readers and a table, none of them large. The wire did not move at
all: no new field type, no schema change beyond "this name is a
string".

Two things came out better than expected. A value read off the wire
comes back TYPED, because the codec knows which class to rebuild - and
a non-member raises at the client rather than reaching libstore. And
the round-trip test says something sharper than "these are valid": it
asserts libstore never answers "unknown" for a declared member, so
succeeding and refusing-because-the-FEATURE-is-off are both fine.
`git` and `blake3` are experimental, and "disabled" is the answer that
proves the word was recognised.
